1. WHAT IS FileInputStream?

FileInputStream is a Java class used to read data from a file as bytes.

Example:

FileInputStream fis =
        new FileInputStream("./JsonFiles/File1.json");

Rest Assured:

given()
        .body(fis)
.when()
        .post(url);

Advantage:
- Simple
- Directly sends JSON file

Disadvantage:
- Cannot easily modify JSON data before sending


==================================================

2. WHAT IS Files.readString()?

Files.readString() is a Java 11 method.

Purpose:
- Reads the complete file content
- Returns it as a String

Example:

String json =
        Files.readString(
                Paths.get("./JsonFiles/File1.json"));

Suppose File1.json contains:

{
   "name":"${name}",
   "email":"${email}"
}

After execution:

json contains:

{
   "name":"${name}",
   "email":"${email}"
}

Now json is a normal Java String.


==================================================

3. WHAT IS Paths.get()?

Paths.get() converts a file location into a Path object.

Example:

Path path =
        Paths.get("./JsonFiles/File1.json");

Here:

"."  = Current Project Directory

"./JsonFiles/File1.json"

means:

CurrentProject
    |
    |-- JsonFiles
            |
            |-- File1.json

Files.readString() requires a Path object.

Correct:

Files.readString(
        Paths.get("./JsonFiles/File1.json"));

Wrong:

Files.readString("./JsonFiles/File1.json");


==================================================

4. WHY USE Files.readString()?

Because String can be modified.

Example:

String json =
        Files.readString(
                Paths.get("./JsonFiles/File1.json"));

json =
        json.replace("${name}",
                     "Sameer");

json =
        json.replace("${email}",
                     "sameer@gmail.com");

Now request body becomes:

{
   "name":"Sameer",
   "email":"sameer@gmail.com"
}

This is not possible directly with FileInputStream.


==================================================

5. DYNAMIC DATA USING FAKER

JSON FILE

{
   "name":"${name}",
   "email":"${email}"
}

Java Code

String json =
        Files.readString(
                Paths.get("./JsonFiles/File1.json"));

json =
        json.replace("${name}",
                     DataGenerator.getName());

json =
        json.replace("${email}",
                     DataGenerator.getEmail());

Now every execution sends unique data.


==================================================

6. WHAT IS JsonPath?

JsonPath is a Rest Assured class.

Used to extract data from JSON Response.

Example Response

{
   "id":123,
   "name":"Sameer",
   "email":"sameer@gmail.com"
}

Code

JsonPath jp =
        response.jsonPath();

int id =
        jp.getInt("id");

String name =
        jp.getString("name");

String email =
        jp.getString("email");


==================================================

